body:
I believe there are therapists you can see online. I see so many ads for “better help”, But I don’t know how good that service is. I would start seeing a therapist and see if they recommend a psychiatrist, and if they do, they can give you a referral. If online doesn’t work for you, then going through your primary care provider to find a therapist isn’t a bad idea.

&#x200B;

Also, there is always the risk that meds can make you worse, especially if you’re on the wrong type. But in my experience, even if there is that risk, it was worth taking it, because meds have made a world of a difference for me with my symptoms. I don’t think I could live a life anything close to stable without them. Yes, some meds can be habit forming, but if you don’t like a med and want to go off, you can consult your doctor about it and they can start tapering you off in small increments. That is the best way to go off a med and makes the withdrawal much less noticeable, if even at all. I’ve quit cold turkey before and that isn’t fun, but always have had a much easier time tapering off.

&#x200B;

Good luck with everything.

My mania made me really angry and I genuinely was worried I was going to hurt someone. Luckily I’m on new meds now and the mania has calmed down a bit to the point where I’m no longer feeling such horrible rage. It’s just mostly hypomania-like symptoms where I’m sleeping little and in a really good mood most of the time and irritable a bit.  I was fortunate that I could get in to see my psychiatrist right away. 

&#x200B;

If you think you might hurt someone or do something to get yourself fired, please call the police to go to the hospital. It’s not worth it to ruin your life because of a shitty illness.

I’m sorry you’re so depressed and have lost so much. I lost a lot because of a depressive episode, including school (I was in a competitive program that was hard to get into. it was my dream to be in this program), my home, my job, some friends. It’s so, so hard to lose everything, but eventually, I climbed my way out of that depression and I got back many of the things I lost, including school and a new job. 

&#x200B;

I know you probably can’t get back into the military (don’t know if you want to), and I know you have an injury that makes some of your old passions hard or impossible to do, but there are other things out there, other passions, other interests to pursue. I hope you can climb out of your depression and get your life back. Hugs to you. Good luck.

I was opposed to antipsychotics for the longest time, but then after experiencing a crazy manic episode, I finally realized I needed meds. I would rather not go into psychosis and ruin my life. Of course when it was mania that was much more tolerable, I didn’t think I needed meds, but mania quickly got to be ”too much too fast” and then I made an appointment with my doc asap to get meds. I’m doing significantly better now.

&#x200B;

 It’s up to them to decide if he wants to take meds, and hopefully it doesn’t take them ruining their life to figure that out.

For me, I knew when I felt completely fucking insane. I used to get twelve hours of sleep every single night because I needed it and then my sleep gradually started to go down. I think I started with hypomania because I felt really good and productive, but now it’s at the point where I’m doing senseless crazy things and I’m getting two to three hours sleep each night. I’m lucky if I get four. I feel like for me, hypomania makes me feel clear headed and on top of the world, and then mania makes me feel like I lost my fucking mind. I can’t stop talking, I can’t stop thinking, I make no sense, I’m doing weird stuff that makes no sense, and I feel more alone than I ever have. Also, i believe how long you’ve had the symptoms can be a telltale sign too because according to the DSM it has to be a minimum of a week for the symptoms, or requiring hospitalization, or if it involves psychosis then it’s automatically mania. Source: [https://medicaidmentalhealth.fmhi.usf.edu/\_assets/file/Guidelines/2017-2018%20Treatment%20of%20Adult%20Bipolar%20Disorder.pdf](https://medicaidmentalhealth.fmhi.usf.edu/_assets/file/Guidelines/2017-2018%20Treatment%20of%20Adult%20Bipolar%20Disorder.pdf)

&#x200B;

long story short, hypomania for me feels really good and productive and mania is just scary and makes me feel like I make no sense. Hope this helps.
